      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:02 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Illinois)",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MADIGAN: Thank you, Justice Stevens, and may it please the Court: This Court has made clear on several occasions, including 21 years ago in Place and 4 years ago in Edmond, that a sniff by a drug-detection dog is not a Fourth Amendment search, and if something is neither a search nor a seizure, then it requires no Fourth Amendment justification."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Did they know in advance that he was someone to look for? Because I don't imagine you arrest everybody on I-80 that goes 70 miles an hour. I've done it many times myself is why -- (Laughter.)"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: He was pulled over for speeding at 71 miles an hour in a 65 miles an hour zone on I-80. Right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. WRAY: Justice Stevens, and may it please the Court: There's no dispute that respondent here was lawfully stopped based on probable cause. There's also no dispute that the entire stop took less than 9 minutes. The question is whether a second officer's use of a drug dog to sniff outside of that car during those 9 minutes required some separate Fourth Amendment justification."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ralph E. Meczyk",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The State does not offer any Fourth Amendment justification whatsoever in regards to -- in this case. It argues instead that there -- there was no need for any justification, and that is incorrect for two reasons. The dog sniff in this case invaded a Fourth Amendment interest of Mr. Caballes in the context of a routine traffic search. The sniff in this case was, in fact, a search. Albeit it was a limited intrusion, it was still a search nonetheless."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why -- why do you say that? I mean, is -- is anything that I observe a search? I mean, suppose I -- I'm a policeman and -- and I'm looking out"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is there no difference between an investigation and a search?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ralph E. Meczyk",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The only way I can analogize that, Justice Kennedy, is that it -- that is akin to a plain smell or plain view."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Suppose a policeman follows me around. He just -- just follows me around, observing with his -- with plain eyes, nothing else. Now, is that a search? Does he need probable cause to do that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ralph E. Meczyk",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It is not a search."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Meczyk",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It invades a private space that in this particular case the respondent Caballes had a privacy interest in, that he wanted to exclude the whole world from going inside his trunk."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: -- if Officer Gillette, the one who did the arrest for speeding, had a dog in the back of his car, instead of having the second officer come with the dog, would it have been permissible? I thought you had conceded that it would be a different situation if the dog was already there when the car stopped."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. MADIGAN",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Second, Justice Scalia asked a question about plain view, and similar to plain view, a dog sniff does not effect an incremental search or seizure. And therefore, similar to plain view, a dog sniff does not require Fourth Amendment justification."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-03-923",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9bc66f11da07fbab65d34e703b9fc174ed690cb8a66b98b1b55a6ea410966d5c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9bc66f11da07-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The -- the case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
